   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public service law, in relation to penalties for the\nfailure of gas and electric corporations to file or correct an annual report",
    "basePrintNo": "S7229",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7229-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 28,
       "fullName": "Liz Krueger",
       "imgName": "401_liz_krueger.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 401,
       "sessionMemberId": 2345,
       "sessionYear": 2025,
       "shortName": "KRUEGER"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §66, Pub Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Public Service Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7229\n\nSPONSOR: GIANARIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public service law, in relation to penalties for the\nfailure of gas and electric corporations to file or correct an annual\nreport\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo ensure compliance with reporting requirements under the Public\nService Law by increasing penalties for any utility company that fails\nto file or correct an annual report with the Public Service Commission.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends subdivision 6 of § 66 of the public service law, as\namended by chapter 784 of the laws of 1923, to increase the penalties\nimposed upon gas and electric utilities for failure to file or correct\nan annual report with the public service commission.\n\nSection two sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nA Task Force convened in response to a massive power outage in western\nQueens in 2006 found that, in many cases, the sanctions imposed by the\nPublic Service Commission are neither timely nor meaningful.  While New\nYork's Public Service Commission is authorized to sanction utility\nproviders for violating deadlines or failing to correct their annual\nreports are not meaningful or imposed in a timely fashion. A $100 penal-\nty is simply a minor cost of doing business to a multi-billion-dollar\nutility provider. The amounts that a major utility could be penalized\nfor being one day, or even one year, late in meeting a deadline would be\nwell beneath its notice. To aggressively protect consumers, the PSC\nneeds to have sufficient tools with which to do so. This bill would\nbring the penalties in line with the realities of today's energy\nmarkets.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2024-23: S172 died in Energy and Telecommunications / no Assembly same\nas\n\n2022: S1244 died in Energy and Telecommunications / no Assembly same as\n\n2021: S1244 advanced to Third Reading / no Assembly same as\n\n2019-20: S2154 died in Energy and Telecommunications / no Assembly same\nas\n\n2017-18: S3234 died in Energy and Telecommunications / no Assembly same\nas\n\n2015-16: S2482 died in Energy and Telecommunications / no Assembly same\nas\n\n\n2013-14: S608 died in Energy and Telecommunications / A647 died in ener-\ngy\n\n2012: S4230 died in Rules / A2364 advanced to third reading\n\n2011: S4230 died in Energy and Telecommunications / A2364 advanced to\nthird reading\n\n2010: A1595 ordered to third reading / no Senate same as\n\n2009: A1595 advanced to third reading / no Senate same as\n\n2008: A8623 advanced to third reading / no Senate same as\n\n2007: A8623 died in codes / no Senate same as\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTBD\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred twentieth day after it\nshall have become a law. Effective immediately, the public service\ncommission is authorized to take any and all actions, including but not\nlimited to the promulgation of any necessary rules, necessary to fully\nimplement the provisions of this act on its effective date.\n",
